USDA FoodData Central — bundled generic food composition data
=============================================================

Source
  U.S. Department of Agriculture, Agricultural Research Service, Beltsville Human
  Nutrition Research Center. FoodData Central. https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/

Licence
  Public domain. FoodData Central states: "USDA FoodData Central data are in the public
  domain and they are not copyrighted. They are published under CC0 1.0 Universal
  (CC0 1.0)." No permission is required for reuse; USDA requests that FoodData Central
  be listed as the source, which is what this file does.

Suggested citation (USDA's own wording)
  U.S. Department of Agriculture, Agricultural Research Service, Beltsville Human
  Nutrition Research Center. FoodData Central. [Internet]. [cited 2026-09-16].
  Available from https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/.

Included in usda-generic.sqlite
  Foundation Foods   release 2026-04-30    469 records
  SR Legacy          release 2018-04     7,793 records
                                         -----
                                         8,262 records

Deliberately excluded
  Branded Foods      428 MB of product-label data whose rows consumed the live search
                     API's result window — a query for "ground beef" returned 25 Branded
                     rows out of 25. Excluding them makes that failure structurally
                     impossible rather than merely filtered.
  FNDDS (Survey)     Measured against a 48-concept benchmark it lifted only two concepts
                     over Foundation + SR Legacy, while adding 5,432 rows (2,710 of them
                     prepared or composite dishes) and inflating candidate sets ~65%.

Provenance and regeneration
  SOURCES.json in this directory records the exact archive filenames and their SHA-256
  digests. Regenerate with:

      python3 scripts/import_usda.py \
        FoodData_Central_foundation_food_csv_2026-04-30.zip \
        FoodData_Central_sr_legacy_food_csv_2018-04.zip

  Every stored value is a verbatim FoodData Central figure. Sodium and cholesterol are
  divided by 1000 (mg -> g) so the units match every other provider; nothing else is
  transformed, and no value is estimated, averaged or hand-authored.
